## 3. Client Work, Collaboration, and Private Infrastructure

You may incorporate the Software into a Permitted Project created for a client and may deliver that completed project, including its source code, to the client.

A client receiving a Permitted Project may use, operate, maintain, modify, and further develop the Software as incorporated into that project, subject directly to this License.

You may provide the Software to employees, contractors, agencies, developers, designers, and other collaborators solely as reasonably necessary for them to work on a Permitted Project.

You may maintain a private or internal modified version of the Software and use it across multiple Permitted Projects, including projects for different clients.

You may maintain and distribute the Software through private source repositories, private package registries, build systems, caches, continuous-integration systems, or other internal development infrastructure solely for use by You and Your collaborators in connection with Permitted Projects.

Private internal distribution under the preceding paragraph does not permit public distribution or the provision of the Software as a Reusable Software Product.

Receiving the Software for work on a Permitted Project does not grant a client or collaborator the right to redistribute or commercialize the Software as a Reusable Software Product.

## 4. Distribution as Part of a Permitted Project

You may distribute compiled, bundled, minified, transformed, or otherwise incorporated portions of the Software as reasonably necessary to deliver a Permitted Project to its users.

You may provide the Software in source form to a client or collaborator when reasonably necessary to create, deliver, operate, maintain, or modify that Permitted Project.

These forms of distribution are expressly permitted.

A public website or web application may therefore deliver compiled or bundled Software to users through ordinary web operation without violating this License.

## 5. Reusable Redistribution Is Prohibited

Except as expressly permitted for a Permitted Project or for private internal infrastructure under Section 3, You may not sell, sublicense, license, rent, lease, republish, repackage, or otherwise distribute the Software, whether modified or unmodified, as a Reusable Software Product.

Without limiting the preceding paragraph, You may not make the Software, a material portion of the Software, or a derivative version of the Software available to third parties as:

- a standalone library or package;
- an npm package or equivalent package-registry distribution;
- a framework;
- a reusable grid or layout system;
- a component library or component system;
- a design-system product;
- a software development kit;
- a reusable template library;
- a reusable starter kit;
- a theme or template product intended for repeated installation or reuse across separate projects;
- a website-building toolkit;
- an application-building toolkit;
- a visual layout or page-building tool;
- a white-label development product;
- a hosted development platform that exposes functionality derived from or incorporating the Software as a reusable capability;
- an API or service that exposes functionality derived from or incorporating the Software for use in third-party projects;
- a paid or unpaid download of the Software itself; or
- a similar reusable software product or service derived from or incorporating the Software.

You may not rename, rebrand, fork, or modify the Software and then distribute the resulting software as Your own reusable library, framework, package, template system, development tool, or product.

Modifications, additional features, different styling, different naming, or additional code do not remove this restriction when the resulting product continues to contain or derive from the Software and makes that functionality available for reuse across projects.

Nothing in this Section prohibits You from independently creating software that does not copy, incorporate, modify, or derive from the Software.

## 6. End-Use Applications and Hosted Services

You may operate a commercial website or web application whose own interface or product experience uses the Software.

You may charge users to access such a website or web application.

For clarity, ordinary SaaS products, editorial sites, commerce sites, portfolios, internal applications, dashboards, and other end-use applications may use the Software as part of their interface.

You may not use the Software to provide third parties with a reusable website-building, application-building, page-building, layout-building, template-building, or development capability for creating separate projects.

A hosted service remains permitted when its own interface uses the Software. The restriction applies when functionality derived from or incorporating the Software is itself made available to third parties as reusable development functionality for creating or supplying other projects.

## 7. Templates, Themes, and Starter Kits

You may create templates, themes, patterns, starter material, and other reusable development material for Your own internal use in Permitted Projects.

You may create and transfer bespoke source code as part of a specific Permitted Project for a client.

You may not sell, publish, distribute, or otherwise make available a reusable template, theme, starter kit, boilerplate, or similar product when the Software or functionality derived from or incorporating the Software forms part of the reusable value of that product.

For clarity:

- selling or transferring a completed website or web application is permitted;
- delivering source code for a specific client project is permitted;
- using an internal starter across Your own client practice is permitted;
- storing an internal starter in a private repository or private package registry is permitted;
- selling the same Unigrid-based starter, theme, or reusable template to multiple customers as a development product is prohibited; and
- operating a marketplace or service that supplies Unigrid-based reusable templates or development artifacts to third parties is prohibited.

## 8. Projects May Be Sold

Nothing in this License prevents You from selling, licensing, transferring, or otherwise commercially exploiting a Permitted Project that incorporates the Software as part of the larger project.

For clarity, You may:

- build a website for a client and charge for it;
- transfer the completed website and its source code to the client;
- build commercial websites using the Software;
- operate a commercial web application using the Software;
- charge users to access a web application incorporating the Software;
- use the Software across multiple client engagements; and
- charge for Your own labor, expertise, design, development, hosting, support, or maintenance.

The restrictions in Sections 5 through 7 apply to commercialization or redistribution of the Software as reusable software or reusable development functionality. They do not prevent commercialization of Permitted Projects created with the Software.

